--TEST--
`Core\Validate::isPrintable` refuses Unicode general category `Cc` and nothing else: both refused runs are found by sweeping rather than named, the invisible characters just outside the category are accepted, and the answer over a string is the conjunction over its characters — docs/spec/01-core-library.md § 12
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `validate-ascii-and-printable-name-their-own-bounds.nvst` names where this
// member's line falls in the first 272 code points. This case asserts what the
// line *is*: general category `Cc`, which is exactly "has no printed form" —
// and not any of the three things it is repeatedly mistaken for. It is not
// "renders visibly" (a bidi override is printable), not "is not a line break"
// (`U+2028` is printable and `\n` is not), and not "is ASCII" (that is the
// neighbouring member).
//
// So each row below names a refused character beside the character just outside
// the category doing the same job, which is the only way a member that grew a
// wider reading of "control" fails: a spoofing check answers every named row in
// the sibling case identically and parts company here.
// `crates/nvs-stdlib/src/validate.rs`'s module doc owns why refusing a bidi
// override would be the false confidence ADR 0024 exists to prevent.
final class Cp {
    // A single code point as a `string`. `Core\Str::fromCodePoint` takes a
    // `uint`, so a sweep's `int` counter is converted at the argument.
    public static function one(int $c): string {
        return Core\Str::fromCodePoint($c as uint);
    }

    public static function yn(bool $b): string {
        return $b ? "y" : "n";
    }
}

// Ending a line is a job two categories do. `LF` (0x0A), `CR` (0x0D) and `NEL`
// (0x85, the C1 one a UTF-8 decode can produce) are `Cc` and are refused;
// `LINE SEPARATOR` (U+2028) and `PARAGRAPH SEPARATOR` (U+2029) do the same job
// from categories `Zl` and `Zp` and are accepted. The pair is the bound: a
// member that asked "does this end a line" rather than "is this `Cc`" answers
// `n` five times here.
echo "line-breaks ",
     Cp::yn(Core\Validate::isPrintable(Cp::one(10))),
     Cp::yn(Core\Validate::isPrintable(Cp::one(13))),
     Cp::yn(Core\Validate::isPrintable(Cp::one(133))),
     Cp::yn(Core\Validate::isPrintable(Cp::one(8232))),
     Cp::yn(Core\Validate::isPrintable(Cp::one(8233))),
     "\n";

// The five characters a spoofing check exists to refuse — `SOFT HYPHEN`,
// `ZERO WIDTH SPACE`, `LEFT-TO-RIGHT MARK`, `RIGHT-TO-LEFT OVERRIDE` and the
// byte order mark. None is `Cc`, so all five are printable, and this member
// says so rather than half-refusing them.
echo "invisible ",
     Cp::yn(Core\Validate::isPrintable(Cp::one(173))),
     Cp::yn(Core\Validate::isPrintable(Cp::one(8203))),
     Cp::yn(Core\Validate::isPrintable(Cp::one(8206))),
     Cp::yn(Core\Validate::isPrintable(Cp::one(8238))),
     Cp::yn(Core\Validate::isPrintable(Cp::one(65279))),
     "\n";

// Separating words is the same story: `SPACE`, `NO-BREAK SPACE` and
// `IDEOGRAPHIC SPACE` are `Zs` and accepted, and `TAB` is `Cc` and refused
// however much it reads like one of them.
echo "spaces ",
     Cp::yn(Core\Validate::isPrintable(Cp::one(32))),
     Cp::yn(Core\Validate::isPrintable(Cp::one(160))),
     Cp::yn(Core\Validate::isPrintable(Cp::one(12288))),
     Cp::yn(Core\Validate::isPrintable(Cp::one(9))),
     "\n";

// Both runs, searched for over `U+0000`..`U+20FF` instead of named: how many
// code points are refused, how many separate runs they form, and where the last
// one ends. `Cc` is 65 code points in two runs — `0x00`..`0x1F`, then `DEL`
// through `0x9F` as one unbroken stretch — ending at `0x9F`. Any wider reading
// of "control" moves at least the first number, and one that treated `DEL` as
// its own case moves the second.
int $refused = 0;
int $runs = 0;
int $last = 0;
bool $previous = true;
int $c = 0;
for (; $c < 8448; $c = $c + 1) {
    bool $ok = Core\Validate::isPrintable(Cp::one($c));
    if (!$ok) {
        $refused = $refused + 1;
        $last = $c;
        if ($previous) {
            $runs = $runs + 1;
        }
    }
    $previous = $ok;
}
echo "sweep ", $refused as string, "|", $runs as string, "|", $last as string, "\n";

// The answer over a `string` is the conjunction over its characters, so one
// character decides it wherever it sits. The same insertion done twice over
// every position of a five-character subject: a `TAB` refuses all six, and a
// `RIGHT-TO-LEFT OVERRIDE` — the character that would break a member reading
// "printable" as "safe to show" — refuses none.
string $base = "novis";
int $withControl = 0;
int $withOverride = 0;
int $p = 0;
for (; $p < 6; $p = $p + 1) {
    string $head = Core\Str::slice($base, 0, $p);
    string $tail = Core\Str::slice($base, $p);
    if (Core\Validate::isPrintable($head . Cp::one(9) . $tail)) {
        $withControl = $withControl + 1;
    }
    if (Core\Validate::isPrintable($head . Cp::one(8238) . $tail)) {
        $withOverride = $withOverride + 1;
    }
}
echo "insert ", $withControl as string, "/6 ", $withOverride as string, "/6\n";

// And the subject with no characters to ask about, which this member answers
// `true` where every `ctype_*` answers `false` — the module doc owns why.
echo "empty ", Cp::yn(Core\Validate::isPrintable("")), "\n";
--EXPECT--
line-breaks nnnyy
invisible yyyyy
spaces yyyn
sweep 65|2|159
insert 0/6 6/6
empty y
